lima: pack the PP's uniforms as the Mali blob does (driver-side, PP only), and give each source of an fmad its own
uniform load. Ours (development record #366 wrote it, #375 checked it on the board and moved it into the image build;
docs/RK3128-LINUX.md "GPU: blob baseline" finding 3 and "GPU: on the board with lima"): on lima at 1920x1080 the packed
case and 100 draws switching between a packed and an unpacked program read back exact, LIMA_DEBUG=dump shows the
uploaded buffer in the packed layout, and the Demo, Games and Photos renders are byte-identical to the build without it.

The ticket's literal fix, packed_uniforms in lima_init_screen_caps, is rejected: with it Mesa lays uniforms out tightly
with no vec4 alignment (gl_nir_link_uniforms.c adds each with pad_and_align = false), so a vec3 or vec4 can straddle two
slots, and the PP's load.u reads one aligned vec4 slot (reference UI's polygon fill: u_e1 would sit at dwords 7-9, across slots 1
and 2). Instead lima keeps the API layout and repacks per fragment shader:
- lima_program.c lima_nir_pack_pp_uniforms: for a shader with only direct uniform loads in its first 32 slots, the used
  components of each slot (from component 0 to the last read) go, widest first, into the first slot with room; loads
  are rewritten to full vec4 loads of the new slot plus a swizzle (folded into the users by copy propagation). The map
  is in lima_fs_compiled_shader.state (so the disk cache keeps it).
- lima_draw.c lima_update_pp_uniform: uploads in the shader's layout, also when only the compiled FS changed.
- lima_nir_duplicate.c: a uniform read by both the mul and the add of an fmad gets a load for each (as load_const does),
  so both read ^uniform in one instruction; ppir shares the slot when the index and size match.
Measured with tools/malitrace/lima-compare.sh --pinned --patch (after dot2-add.patch) on capture 20261106T175604Z: Demo
63 -> 61 PP instructions (program 1 3 -> 2 = blob, packing; program 22 5 -> 4, the fmad loads alone); reference UI unchanged
(its uniforms are vec4s and vec3s with nothing to fill them); on its own, without dot2-add.patch, the fmad loads also take
the reference UI polygon fill from 15 to 11. Off the board, LIMA_DEBUG=dump of a draw through lima's
no-op drm-shim shows the uploaded fp16 buffer in the packed layout the shader reads. Applies to 26.2.6 + 0001-0003
(without dot2-add.patch, which #375 left out of the build) and, on its own or after dot2-add.patch, to Mesa main.

